#
# The filtered variable is a parameterized rule's parameter, and the filter walks the map's entries.
# Over the `Metadata` map of single-map-binding-without-type-template.yaml, which holds only the
# authentication block (no scalar entry, and `testBasic` is not a key of the map itself), the filter
# keeps the walk. The one entry carries `testBasic`, so `testBasic !exists` rejects it and nothing is
# kept, while the predicate holds on the map read as one value, because the map lacks the key. The
# selection is lost, so the rule's `when` gate does not apply: `has_no_test_basic` is SKIP and the caller
# FILTER_THROUGH_A_PARAMETER reports SKIP (exit 0). There is no `let` to name, so the notice names the
# parameter and the rule, and the query the call site bound it to.
#
# The same call with a predicate that names the map's own key is one object and is tested as a whole
# (filter_through_a_parameter.guard), with no notice. The same filter written in the rule's body instead
# of its `when` is walking_filter_in_a_parameterized_rules_body.guard.
#
rule has_no_test_basic(m) when %m[ testBasic !exists ] !empty {
  %m exists
}

rule FILTER_THROUGH_A_PARAMETER {
  has_no_test_basic(Resources.Metadata)
}
